.TH RUN-APPSCAN 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-appscan \- weekly application-security report to the App Dev audience, and scanner-login faults to the operator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-appscan
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real | \-\-to\-dev )
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-appscan
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real | \-\-to\-dev )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-appscan
renders the week's Qualys WAS scan results with
.BR cti\-appscan (1)
and mails them through
.BR cti\-mailer (1)
.BR "\-\-lane was" ,
to the people who own application code rather than the digest audience.
No model is involved.
.PP
It is run by
.B cti\-agent\-appscan.service
from
.B cti\-agent\-appscan.timer
every Friday at 07:30
.RB ( Persistent=true ),
half an hour after the weekly rollup. The unit runs as
.BR ctiagent ,
with
.B cti\-agent\-alert@.service
on failure.
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 1. Prepare
Checks that
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-appscan
and
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-mailer
are executable (fatal if not), sets
.I $FLEET_HOME/state
to 0700, creates today's HTML and text files empty at 0600 before anything
is written to them, and deletes today's authentication-failure file, whose
absence after the run is itself a signal.
.TP
.B 2. Report
.B cti\-appscan \-\-out \-\-text\-out \-\-auth\-fail\-out \-\-seen\-out \-\-tickets\-out \-\-ticket\-slot
reads the scan notifications in the CTI mailbox, the WAS findings API and
the KnowledgeBase's fix for each QID, and writes the report and each
application's ticket input. Fatal on a non-zero exit.
.TP
.B 3. Subject
.B cti\-appscan \-\-subject\-only
is run as a second, separate collection. Fatal if it fails or prints
nothing.
.TP
.B 3a. Application tickets (non-fatal)
.B cti\-jira \-\-appscan\-tickets
with the tickets file and
.BR \-\-ref\-out ,
plus
.B \-\-for\-real
on a real run only - a dry run or
.B \-\-to\-dev
previews. Then
.B cti\-appscan \-\-stamp\-tickets
adds a JIRA TICKETS section to the report naming each application's ticket.
Before the send so the email names them; a Jira failure becomes "not
updated this run" and the email still goes.
.TP
.B 3b. Scanner reports (non-fatal, off by default)
Only with
.B WAS_ATTACH_REPORT=1
and on a real run, or on
.B \-\-to\-dev
as a rehearsal: the reports are fetched (created and deleted in Qualys, as
on a real run) so the preview file
.I appscan\-tickets\-preview\-DATE.txt
names each attachment and its size, and nothing is attached.
.B \-\-dry\-run
does not fetch them (it creates nothing in Qualys) and says so.
Before step 3a files anything, runs
.B cti\-jira \-\-appscan\-tickets
as a preview to a plan file, then
.B cti\-appreport \-\-tickets \-\-only\-ref
with that plan and
.B \-\-out\-dir
under
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state ,
so only applications whose ticket will be filed or commented get a report.
.B cti\-jira
then runs with
.BR "\-\-for\-real \-\-appscan\-reports" ,
and the reports directory and plan are deleted afterwards: the reports
carry session cookies. A failure, or a missing
.BR cti\-appreport ,
is logged and the tickets go out without a report.
.TP
.B 4. Send
.B cti\-mailer \-\-lane was
with the HTML, text and subject, plus
.B \-\-dry\-run
on a dry run. The lane resolves
.B WAS_TO
against
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
and refuses if either is unset; it never falls back to the digest audience.
Fatal on failure.
.TP
.B 5. Scanner logins (non-fatal)
After the report has gone: if the authentication-failure file is absent,
the binary is older than
.B \-\-auth\-fail\-out
and the runner says nothing was checked. If it is empty, every configured
scanner credential worked. If it has lines, the applications are logged
and, on a real run,
.B cti\-alert \-\-unit cti\-agent\-appscan.service \-\-kind HOLD
mails the operator naming them and the file. A missing or failing
.B cti\-alert
is logged; the lane still succeeds.
.PP
A dry run still reads the mailbox and the scanner API, and writes its
report under
.I dryrun/appscan/
(see DRY RUNS AND THE LANE LOCK), never over the real run's. It sends nothing through
.BR cti\-mailer ,
and it logs, but does not alert on, authentication failures.
.SH HOW TO RUN
\fBrun-appscan\fR mails the weekly report and, with WAS_TICKETS=1, files tickets, so it has no default: it has to be told how to run. Give exactly one of
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR (render and print, send and file nothing), \fB\-\-for\-real\fR (send the report and file or update the tickets),
or \fB\-\-to\-dev\fR (a test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only; it may be combined with \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR to rehearse the test send).
With none it prints a usage message and exits 2 without doing anything, and
two that contradict each other are refused the same way. This is the rule for
every fleet command that changes something, whether a person, a systemd unit
or the heartbeat runs it, so a unit that lost its flag fails loudly instead of
quietly doing nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
Shell arguments, in any order.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Send the report. One of this, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR or \fB\-\-to\-dev\fR is required.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Render, print the subject, and pass
.B \-\-dry\-run
to the mailer; no alert is sent.
.TP
.B \-\-to\-dev
A test send: the report goes through
.B cti\-mailer \-\-to\-dev
to
.B FLEET_DEV_EMAIL
only, subject prefixed
.BR "[DEV TEST]" .
.B WAS_TO
and
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
are not consulted, so the report can be seen before the lane's audience is
configured. An authentication-failure alert goes through
.B cti\-alert \-\-to\-dev
to the same address. With
.B \-\-dry\-run
as well, nothing is mailed.
.PP
Anything else is refused with exit status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is sourced with every assignment exported, and its values replace the
process environment, except
.B FLEET_HOME
and
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
which keep the value the unit or wrapper injected. The Go binaries read
their credentials only from the environment, so this is how they arrive.
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
State directory.
Default:\~\fB$HOME\fR (not \fI$HOME/fleet\fR); the unit sets
\fI/var/lib/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_CODE
Code directory.
Default:\~\fB$FLEET_HOME\fR; the unit sets \fI/opt/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_ENV
The config file.
Default:\~\fI$FLEET_HOME/fleet.env\fR.
.TP
.BR WAS_TO ", " WAS_ALLOW_TO
The audience and its allowlist, read by
.B cti\-mailer
(section 4, "application security"). No fallback.
.TP
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
Where
.B cti\-alert
sends an authentication fault (section 4).
.TP
.B WAS_ATTACH_REPORT
.B 1
attaches each application's own scanner report to its Jira ticket (step 3b).
Anything else leaves it off.
Default:\~\fB0\fR.
.TP
.BR APPSCAN_REPORT_PROVIDER ", " QUALYS_RL_RESERVE ", " QUALYS_RL_MAX_WAIT
Read by
.BR cti\-appreport (1).
.PP
Graph and Qualys credentials (sections 1 and 2) are read by
.BR cti\-appscan (1).
.SH FILES
Under
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/state ,
directory mode 0700.
.I DATE
is the UTC date.
.TP
.IR appscan\-DATE.html ", " appscan\-DATE.txt
The report, 0600. A second run on the same day replaces them.
.TP
.IR appscan\-tickets\-DATE.json ", " appscan\-tickets\-ref\-DATE.json
Each application's ticket input, and what
.B cti\-jira
did with it; 0600, deleted at the start of the next run.
.TP
.IR appscan\-tickets\-plan\-DATE.json ", " appscan\-reports\-DATE/
With
.BR WAS_ATTACH_REPORT=1 :
the preview plan and the fetched reports (directory 0700, files 0600).
Deleted as soon as the tickets are done.
.TP
.I appscan\-authfail\-DATE.tsv
One line per scan whose configured credential failed, application first;
written 0600 by
.BR cti\-appscan .
Present and empty: checked, nothing failed. Absent: nothing was checked.
.TP
.I $FLEET_HOME/dryrun/appscan/
A dry run's or test send's files, replaced by the next one.
.TP
.I locks/appscan.lock
The lane lock.
.SH DRY RUNS AND THE LANE LOCK
A dry run and a test send
.RB ( \-\-to\-dev ,
with or without
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
write nothing a real run reads. They work under
.I $FLEET_HOME/dryrun/
(0700), emptied at the start of each one; nothing in the fleet reads it.
Its report, subject, SEEN list, ticket input, refs, plan, preview and
fetched scanner reports go in
.IR dryrun/appscan/ ;
the real run's
.I state/appscan\-*\-DATE
files are neither deleted nor replaced.
.PP
Each run takes the lane lock,
.I state/locks/appscan.lock,
with
.BR flock (2).
A dry run or test send that finds it held \- a timer run, or another
person's rehearsal \- says which process holds it and exits 75 without doing
anything; it never waits and never runs beside the real run. A real run
waits for it up to
.B FLEET_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS
(from
.IR fleet.env ,
default 600, under the unit's
.BR TimeoutStartSec ),
then, if another real run still holds it, fails with exit status 1, so the
unit fails and it can be rerun; if a rehearsal still holds it, goes ahead
with a warning, because a rehearsal writes only its own files. The
kernel drops the lock when the process ends, however it ends, so there is
no stale lock to clear.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was sent (or rendered, on a dry run). Authentication failures
and alerting problems do not change this.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown or extra argument;
.B cti\-appscan
or
.B cti\-mailer
is missing;
.B cti\-appscan
failed or produced an empty subject; or the send failed, including the
mailer's refusal when
.B WAS_TO
or
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
is unset.
.TP
.B 75
A dry run or test send refused because another run of the lane holds
the lane lock. Nothing was done; run it again when that run has finished.
.SH EXAMPLES
Render this week's report and check who it would go to:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-appscan \-\-dry\-run
sudo less /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-$(date \-u +%F).txt
.EE
.PP
See whether a scanner login failed:
.PP
.EX
sudo cat /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-authfail\-$(date \-u +%F).tsv
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-appscan (1),
.BR cti\-appreport (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 11f; fleet\-kit/README.md, "The weekly AppSec report".
